<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- The other half of the perimeter DET-COUVERTURE named. tests/coverage.runsettings is
     read by the Linux job and measures Rempart.Core; this file is read by the Windows job
     and measures Rempart.Windows, which the Linux job cannot even compile
     (net10.0-windows). Until it existed, the 2 712 lines of P/Invoke — the layer whose
     failure mode is "a plausible, wrong value" — were outside every denominator.

     Two files rather than one with a wider Include, deliberately: a single report merging
     Core and Windows would produce one percentage over two layers tested by two suites on
     two operating systems, and nobody could say which half moved. The published summaries
     stay separate for the same reason.

     Coverage is measured here and never gated, exactly as on the other side. The six
     reasons are in docs/DEBT.md and none of them is weakened by this file: adding Windows
     to the measurement widens what is *seen*, it does not turn an indicator into a door.

     The friendlyName below must match the collector name .github/workflows/ci.yml asks
     for. A mismatch is not an error — the collector falls back to its defaults, the
     Include filter disappears, and the figure moves for a reason nobody can name.
     CoverageSettingsTests holds the spellings of both files against the workflow. -->
<RunSettings>
  <DataCollectionRunSettings>
    <DataCollectors>
      <DataCollector friendlyName="XPlat code coverage">
        <Configuration>
          <Format>cobertura</Format>

          <!-- Rempart.Windows only, and the omission of Rempart.Cli is a fact rather than
               an oversight: Rempart.Tests.Windows does not reference Rempart.Cli, so the
               CLI assembly is never loaded by this run and asking for it would only add a
               name to a filter that matches nothing. What it would take to measure it is
               written in the DET-COUVERTURE entry.

               Rempart.Core is left out too, though this suite does execute it: it is
               already measured by the Linux job against the whole fixture replay. Counting
               it a second time here would publish a much lower Core figure next to the
               real one and invite a comparison between two numbers that answer different
               questions. -->
          <Include>[Rempart.Windows]*</Include>

          <!-- Same reason as on the Core side: without this, generated property accessors
               join the denominator and the figure reports how many DTO fields were touched
               rather than how much of the interop layer was exercised. -->
          <SkipAutoProps>true</SkipAutoProps>

          <ExcludeByAttribute>Obsolete,GeneratedCodeAttribute,CompilerGeneratedAttribute</ExcludeByAttribute>

          <!-- The attribute filter above is not enough here, and the difference was
               measured rather than assumed. ComInterfaceGenerator emits the COM vtable
               stubs for the Task Scheduler and WMI interfaces into obj/, and coverlet
               counted every one of them: 751 of 1 677 lines, covered at 4.7 %, dragging the
               figure for this layer from 60.6 % down to 35.5 %. Nine of the twelve
               least-covered "files" were generator output.

               That is precisely the number DET-COUVERTURE must not publish: it moves when
               the generator changes its emission strategy, and it would reward deleting an
               interface rather than testing one. What is measured now is the code someone
               wrote and can be held to. -->
          <ExcludeByFile>**/obj/**/*.cs</ExcludeByFile>

          <IncludeTestAssembly>false</IncludeTestAssembly>
          <SingleHit>false</SingleHit>
          <UseSourceLink>false</UseSourceLink>
          <DeterministicReport>false</DeterministicReport>
        </Configuration>
      </DataCollector>
    </DataCollectors>
  </DataCollectionRunSettings>
</RunSettings>
